+0.14 to +0.26), about +2.7\u00b0F since 1890. College Station's average high shows no clear change: +0.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.06 to +0.13) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.90\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.64 to +1.17) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.64\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.24 to +1.03). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 10 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"College Station's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.04 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 1968 (61.04 in); the driest, 1917 (16.66 in). College Station almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.5 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 3 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}